  The attempt holds its token count and its protocol and still
  loses data, from EITHER side, for the same reason: it does not
  control when its own latch closes.
    trigger 1  a sender that turns round quickly -- the stage
               upstream reopens before this one has closed
    trigger 2  a consumer slow to drop its acknowledge -- the
               outgoing request cannot rise, so the latch cannot
               close at all
  Slack on either side hides it, which is why a hand-driven
  sweep found only half of it.  See verify/attempts/.

  At slow max, BD_ROUTE_PS=0, trigger 1 does not fire; it needs
  the short arcs of the fast-min corner (-DBD_FAST_MIN).
  Trigger 2 survives every regime.  It is structural, and it is
  the reason this cell can never ship.
